esting  and then describes some of the various  technologies that are available and  commonly used  in drug testing i'll provide some  examples of several clinical scenarios  where drug testing is commonly requested  and then describe some approaches that  you can use to design your drug test  panels to ensure that they meet the  various clinical needs  let's get started so there are many  important decisions that need to be made  to ensure that your drug  testing program meets your stated goals  and is successful  and so an important first step then is  to understand how the urine testing  process works  what the testing cannon cannot tell you  so we'll get started by reviewing some  basic context  or concepts in drug testing  so a toxicology screen is a test that  checks for drugs or other chemicals in  your blood  urine or saliva um when i say blood i'm  referring to serum or plasma which is  what's most commonly tested  a top screen can check for one  particular drug or for many different  drugs all at once and so this can  include prescription medications  over-the-counter drugs like aspirin or  things like vitamins  supplement alcohol and even illicit  drugs like cocaine  there are four general categories of  toxicology testing  there is forensic toxicology testing  which is conducted by a laboratory for  medical legal purposes  there's workplace drug testing which is  often done pre-employment as part of the  interview process  or after someone's already been hired on  as random drug testing to  enforce a drug-free workplace there's  athletic drug testing  which typically looks for banned  substances or performance enhancing  agents and this is  typically done in competitive level  athletes and then there's clinical drug  testing  which is conducted to assist in patient  diagnosis and treatment  so my talk today is going to focus on  this last category clinical drug testing  so i'd like to give you some examples of  common scenarios  clinical scenarios where 