ct on it  so their reports are published annually  and then they also have a very nice  summary if you don't have the  the um the time to read the full report  as to what trends that they noticed and  so  i've highlighted the four trends for  2020 and i can  i found it interesting that fentanyl  still remains to be the most lethal drug  in many of these undue sites  despite that they're geographically  scattered  another great resource is the samhsa  website so the substance abuse and  mental health services administration  so they have a variety of reports and  tools that are available on that website  one of them is the national survey on  drug use and health and so  an example of some of the data that they  have there is  this shows heroin use in the past year  among adults and so you can see kind of  like in a heat map  which areas herein uses most prevalent  they also uh have reports from dawn so  this is a drug abuse and warning network  and so this shows trends and ed visits  involving illicit drugs and so  it doesn't mean that taking this illicit  drug was the reason why the person was  admitted to the ed or presented to the  ed  it just means that when they were at the  ed and got a drug test  they screened positive for these tests  and so it's interesting to see  what drugs are being mostly abused the  most commonly abused  so you can also gather information on  the local level  from these federal websites or these  national websites so  the nida website um they also allow you  to drill down  and get summaries by your state for some  things and so this is an example of  opioid summaries that are provided by at  the state level  you get information like this so the  first image on the top left  of the united states you can see at the  heat map the opioid involved overdose  death rates across the country and as  you can see  ohio which is where i'm located we are  in the heart of this  opioid epidemic we can then so this we  knew but then it's helpful to be able to  break this down  and look and see okay well these are the  number of drug overdose deaths involving  opioids in ohio  broken down by prescription opioids  synthetic opioids  heroin that's purchased so that's very  helpful to see  we can also look at things like what are  the prescription rates so in ohio we see  that  ohio providers ordered just slightly  more prescriptions or or  wrote slightly more prescriptions than  the average across the u.s  yet it's disproportionate to the number  of overdose deaths that we're seeing  we also can gather information about  admission rates for  substance abuse disorders into treatment  facilities so we can see what drugs are  most commonly being abused and people  are being  admitted to these treatment facilities  for  you may want to also look to see what  type of local  drug use and abuse surveillance  resources are available so we have  something in ohio called the ohio  substance abuse monitoring network  now this is a network that publishes  these pamphlets or these reports about  twice a year  and i find it to be particularly helpful  because it not only breaks it down  for just the state of ohio but by region  so i'm in the cleveland region  and based on this report what i could  see is that fentanyl and marijuana  and methamphetamine availability has  increased while ecstasy availability has  decreased  and from what they're seeing the  consensus is that fentanyl is easier to  obtain than heroin on the streets here  and so that's helpful information for us  and that's what guided us to realize we  have to add fentanyl  to our screen so the national data  coupled with the  local data helped us to understand that  this is really something that we have to  incorporate into our drug panel  and so in the next month or so we will  have incorporated it into our panel  in 22 sites across northeast ohio  i think it's great that they also  provide  more detailed information like what the  current availability is of prescription  drugs as well as  street drugs what the changes in the  availability so whether it's decreased  or increased since the last six month  period  and of those drug categories which  particular drug is the most  commonly available so for example for  prescription drugs  apparently percocet is the most commonly  available  in our area  i also found it particularly interesting  and helpful that the  they also report the most commonly used  cutting agents  that were determined by the crime lab so  we see that for heroin that it's most  often cut with fentanyl  and tramadol in our area and we see that  very regularly so this is also a reason  why we're adding them to our drug  screens  all right so what tips what additional  tips can i give you for keeping your  drug testing panels relevant  well i would regularly check these  national and local surveillance data  tools that i just mentioned  um you may also want to consider  crunching your own positivity rates over  several years  and consider removing drug screens that  don't add any value  you may want to review reference lab  positivity rate data as well so i will  tell you that we were  playing around with the idea of removing  pcp from our panel because  we hadn't seen a positive pcp screen in  a really long time there were very very  few over a three year period  but after consulting with our clinical  toxicologist and seeing information like  this which i'm showing here in the table  uh or the image to the right  that there are actually small pockets in  ohio where pcp is still being abused  it would not be smart for us to remove  it from our panel and so you can  review reference lab positivity data to  see what they're seeing in your area and  see if that aligns with what you're  seeing and this might be particularly  helpful if you're a smaller lab that  doesn't have a very high drug  testing volume so the image on the right  is from quest diagnostics  and it's showing the pcp positivity  rates based on  zip code and it's very hard to see here  but it does show the pockets of  cities in ohio where pcp is still being  used which correlates to what our  clinical toxicologist was also  mentioning  i'd also continue seeking input from  treatment professionals so your  colleagues  you can consider reaching out to the  medical examiner's office  law enforcement in your area and i also  find instrument and assay vendors to be  very helpful for consulting for things  like this they typically have their  fingers on the pulse of what's going on  what's new what's coming out there so  that  they can proactively create commercially  available tests to help us to fight  these pandemics  or epidemics i will always recommend to  attend professional conferences and  seminars to stay up to date on the new  drugs that are coming out  or the new technologies that are  available to help you detect them  and if you are willing to travel down  the rabbit hole with me this last one  is quite interesting it's checking web  forums like bluelight.org  opiophile or reddit threads just to name  a few for open discussions about drug  use  and so it's not a bunch of people  talking about how great drugs are and  trying to promote drug use it's really  intended  um to reduce the harm so that people  will if they are going to use drugs that  they do it more safely  and can have their questions answered  so why would i ever recommend that you  you read these  well there's data and evidence that  shows that social listening data can be  really helpful for monitoring drug use  and misuse and so i suspect that there  will be additional reports that we'll  see  coming out based on what people are  finding by by using social listening to  these threads  so in summary the critical  a critical first step to ensuring that  your drug testing program meets your  stated goals and is successful  is to understand how the testing process  works and what it can and cannot tell  you  i hope that i've demonstrated to you  that drug testing is not a  one-size-fits-all process that there are  varying needs for different patient  populations  and that you really need to consider the  test characteristics that are needed  and i don't just mean analytical i also  mean the clinical and the operational  i encourage you to collaborate with  other departments to develop your  institutional testing guidelines  and to create educational materials to  help them know which test to order in  which scenarios  i'd also encourage you to collaborate  with them to learn about their  experiences on the front line since they  are the first to  see new prescription drugs coming to the  market know how frequently they're  prescribing them  or maybe they're seeing more illicit  drug use that they can tell you that  that something is about to be big in our  area  i also recommend regularly reviewing  these national and local drug use abuse  and overdose trends  and evaluate them against your current  panel to see what you can improve what  you might need to consider adding  removing  and i just want to emphasize that  optimizing your drug testing panel  is an iterative process you can't just  set it up and walk away and never go  back to it  you must regularly go back and check to  see what the climate is around you what  the environment is  what's coming your way and then react  accordingly  so with that i'd like to thank you all  for your time  i'd like to thank thermal fisher  scientific for sponsoring this webinar  and i am happy to take questions  thank you jamie for your informative  presentation  we will now go ahead and start the live  q a portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen and we'll answer as  many questions as we have time for  okay let's go ahead and get started your  first  question is  which chromatography technique is best  for drug testing  that's a harder question to answer which  one is best i think it depends on what  drug you're trying to test for so some  drugs are inherently more  volatile than others others you just  will have a really hard time trying to  derivatize them in order to get them to  be label enough for gc to be the good  method so i think it depends on what  panel  you're talking about i think gcms has  historically been the gold standard for  confirmation testing but i think more  and more people are moving to lcms  because it tends to be more robust  so in the sense that you can test for  more drugs without having to derivatize  them  and you can work with a range of  polarities  i hope that adequately answered the  question  thank you okay next question can  immunoassays discern  between delta 9 thc and delta 8  thc i wouldn't  i i don't believe so i mean but i to be  honest i  i don't i don't know the answer to that  one i know that in general  amino acids the antibodies are designed  to  recognize the 3d and chemical structures  and those sound like they are very close  to each other  they will probably be able to detect  both if it's able to detect it  but maybe at different concentrations  but those are very structurally similar  okay all right your next question  can you detect lsd in a urine sample  i'm sure you could detect lsata urine  tip i don't know that anyone is really  using lsd much anymore  but yes  now is the immunoassay used for  drug screen a laboratory test or a poct  well it could be either so it depends on  how you choose to use it  in our system i can tell you that we  have some physician offices that have a  point of care  here in drug screen where they're either  in a cassette based form or a cup  cup form and then we also have one  that's done in the laboratory so on our  larger chemistry analyzer so  the screen can be either but the  confirmation is always the same in our  system so we build it so that they will  all screens that are well i should say  we built all of them but screens that  are positive  always get reflex to our uh lcms  confirmation in our laboratory so there  are several different screening  modalities that are used  okay i've seen this question a few times  um the attendees want to know if the  recording is going to be available  for them to view later and i do want to  let you know that this event will be on  demand  you will be able to to view the webinar  again after it's finished today  okay let's go ahead and move on to our  next question what is the best test for  alcohol  best test um in my time i feel like  it's evolved i think now people are  using ethyl glucuronide more  though i don't know how many labs  actually have it on site  so i will say that in our laboratory we  just test for ethanol  um but we do have several like addiction  recovery services  um and several other specialties that we  send out for ethyl glucuronide  i believe that that is a better marker  we would like to eventually bring that  in house but we have not done so yet and  i know that there are also  several other markers that i see  coming up that would be helpful but i  don't think that they have been adopted  as widely  in in the general clinical lab setting  great thank you now what cut what  cut for amp and benzo is recommended  i'm sorry i don't think i understand the  question yeah this user says  what cut of 4 amps and benzo is  recommended oh what cutoff  what cutoff for amp and benzo is  recommended oh okay  got it um so i think that depends on  what you're using it for  i would say in general for the workplace  drug testing the cutoffs are much higher  but um in the clinical setting it's  quite a bit lower so  i could tell you that we use for our  amphetamine uh screen we use a 500  nanogram per ml cut off  and um a 200 nanogram per ml cut off for  benzodiazepines  so i think that for clinical testing i  would use the  the um the lower cut off so that you're  able to detect more  um if you're doing it for workplace  testing the higher cutoff is better  because you don't want to  pick up on people who are taking things  that they're prescribed you're looking  for  them taking drugs that they're abusing  okay great thank you i think we have  time for a few more questions here next  question  is can screen and confirmation both be  done  on lcms that's a great question  so yes they can actually both be done on  lcms i think that few institutions have  the ability to do that  because lcms is not so common that it's  in every lab these days  but yes they can both be done there are  several institutions that are doing that  now  and just bypassing the immunoassay  screen altogether  so i think that that's probably where  the field is moving towards but i think  that until  more labs can get their hands on lcms  can can have the expertise on site i  think that immunosa will be around for a  while  a very good question great thing thank  you  now if a patient is using cough syrups  or sleeping pills will we get the  positive result  in drug testing  you can you can you get a false positive  or sometimes a real a true positive if  it's an amphetamine  containing medication on a urine drug  screen but that's where the confirmation  testing comes into play to help you  discern whether or not it's a false  positive or true positive  now what about integrity checks  great question so there are a number of  things that you can do to check the  integrity of a specimen i will tell you  that  in-house we have chosen to do a  creatinine check  and this is not for all tests this we've  only included this in our opiate  benzodiazepine prescription compliance  panel  but we'll do a creatinine check to make  sure that it's at a certain threshold  to ensure appropriate concentration of  the urine  um if it is less than that threshold we  will automatically reflex a specific  gravity testing  now i know that there's some labs that  will also look for  adulterants and things like that but we  are not currently doing that in-house  we used to send that out to a reference  lab so that we could have it available  to our clinician should they feel that  it's necessary to do that but we don't  do it in-house  so just creatinine and specific gravity  is what we do in our clinical lab  thank you now this question says  we have a client that takes byvance and  is  positive for amp and manp every urine  test  how do i determine whether he is  actually abusing meth  his drug of choice  so there are higher level methods that  can be used to detect  street amphetamine from what would be  prescribed um we don't run any of those  in-house so you're saying that his  drug of choice is methamphetamine  correct sorry i want to make sure i  heard that correctly yes  correct okay well we can tell the  difference between methamphetamine and  amphetamine in these  these tests um we can also tell the  difference between the dnl  isomers of methamphetamine to determine  whether or not it's coming from an  illicit source versus a prescribed  source  i'm assuming then that this patient has  also been positive for methamphetamine  at some point  and you can differentiate  methamphetamine from amphetamine in your  confirmation test  okay thank you now heroin has a very  small window of testing  what can i do to extend it  i don't know how you can extend the  window i do understand where the  question is coming from that  the very specific metabolite to  heroin acetyl morphine um that's very  labile very volatile doesn't exist very  long but you can detect the morphine  and hydromorphone for quite a bit of  time after use  we're also seeing that a lot of heroin  is cut with tramadol and fentanyl  at least in our population so we're able  to see a very clear pattern of  morphine hydromorphone fentanyl and its  metabolites  so while you you won't be able to detect  that 6am  for very long you can detect the  other metabolites of heroin  further down the pathway  thanks so much jamie we have time for  one more question here  from a technical staffing issue are you  running  lcms testing 24 7  and if so at how many locations in your  system  ah very good question i would love to be  able to write it 24 7 but unfortunately  we don't have the staffing or expertise  to do that  it's been challenging to even bring the  lcms testing in-house because  it's not something that's taught in in  med tech school so we had to  kind of train the staff ourselves to do  this so right now we offer it  only on first shift the intention is to  expand as we bring on other types of  testing to  run it across at least two shifts right  now we're running it only five days a  week  and because of the equipment and the  expertise that's needed  we have concentrated all of our testing  to one site on campus so we are kind of  the mothership hospital we have the  largest hospital in our system  and we receive all of the work from the  12 community hospitals around us  and all of the physician offices that  use our testing services  i can tell you that one of the things  that we have done in order to kind of  streamline this process  is we ensure that people use the same  sample cup  we have special cups that we have them  used so we can sample from directly  onto our sample prep instrument so we've  done things to kind of streamline the  process and still have a great  turnaround time  despite that we're doing the testing for  our whole system in just one location  well thank you again jamie for your time  today and your important research  we would also like to thank labyrus and  our sponsor thermo fisher scientific  for underwriting today's educational  webcast and as a reminder before we go  i would like to thank the audience for  joining us today and for their questions  any questions we did not have time for  today and those submitted during the  on-demand period  will be addressed by our speaker via the  contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast